var tafsirData_54 = {
  "1": "“The Hour has drawn near” continues the urgency at the end of the previous chapter. Final judgment is certain and approaching, while its exact time remains with Allah. Muhammad’s final prophethood and the signs shown through him announce that history is moving toward its account.\n\n“And the moon was split in two” refers to the miracle shown during Muhammad’s life when Meccan opponents requested or witnessed a sign. Authentic reports from multiple Companions describe the moon appearing split into two parts and then restored by Allah’s command.\n\nThe event is not explained away merely as a future metaphor because the verse uses completed wording and the next verse describes people seeing a sign and dismissing it. Allah who created the moon can alter it without difficulty.\n\nBelievers should not support the miracle with fabricated photographs, space-agency rumors, or unsupported scientific claims. Its evidence comes through Quran and authentic transmission. Nor does it provide a date for the Hour. The sign established Muhammad’s truthfulness, but determined deniers called it magic. The moral question is whether signs produce humility. The split moon points beyond celestial wonder toward the near Judgment for which every observer must prepare.\n\nThe moon’s restoration also shows that a miracle is not necessarily a permanent disruption. Allah may display a sign for witnesses and return creation to its normal order. Demanding that every miracle leave a modern measurable scar misunderstands its purpose and historical evidence. Believers need not fear honest astronomy; astronomy describes the moon as observed and cannot adjudicate every brief event in the past without surviving physical data. Faith here rests on transmitted revelation. The strongest defense refuses fake evidence, studies authentic reports, and lets the miracle point to the message rather than become detached spectacle. The authentic sign needs no fabricated support to remain true and spiritually compelling.",
  "2": "“Whenever they see a sign, they turn away” describes a settled pattern, not lack of evidence. The moon’s splitting joins the Quran and other proofs, yet each sign becomes another occasion for refusal. Physical sight cannot guide a heart committed to turning.\n\n“Same old magic!” is their dismissive label. They place a new miracle into an old accusation used against earlier messengers. Calling it magic allows them to acknowledge something unusual while denying divine meaning. “Same old” also suggests they see prophetic signs as a repeated trick rather than a repeated mercy.\n\nThe verse does not instruct Muslims to label every skeptical question obstinate. Evidence should be presented accurately, and unusual claims require verification. Here Allah reveals the response to an actual sign.\n\nBelievers should avoid weak stories that make dismissal easier. The Quran and authentic reports are sufficient. They also examine their own habit of turning away from moral signs. One may affirm miracles while ignoring prayer, justice, and repentance. Genuine belief lets signs redirect conduct. Magic accusations changed no reality; the moon, Messenger, and approaching Hour remained under Allah’s truth. A label protects pride only temporarily, while the account comes nearer.\n\n“Whenever” suggests a general habit extending beyond one miracle. If evidence appeared, they assigned magic; if warning came, they called it old. A refusal with infinitely movable standards cannot be satisfied. Believers should beware similar habits when evaluating criticism of themselves: if every outcome is reinterpreted to preserve innocence, learning stops. Set fair standards before evidence appears and apply them consistently. The pagans’ phrase was socially convenient but intellectually empty. A sign’s repetition across prophetic history does not make it stale magic; it makes Allah’s mercy and human resistance recurring realities. The honest response to repeated evidence is reconsideration, not a repeated label designed to block every possible conclusion.",
  "3": "“They rejected the truth” states the response beneath their magic accusation. The moon’s sign and Muhammad’s message were not dismissed after neutral investigation; truth was refused. Rejection becomes morally serious when evidence has arrived and desire controls the verdict.\n\n“Followed their own desires” identifies the competing authority. Desire itself is created and can serve lawful good. The problem is making it the guide over revelation, accepting only what protects status, idols, and habit.\n\n“Every matter will be settled” promises stability after conflict. Truth and falsehood, the Messenger’s mission and opponents’ schemes, deeds and consequences will reach their appointed outcome. Public confusion does not last forever.\n\nThe verse encourages honest self-examination. A person can call a conclusion rational while desire selected it first. Ask whether the same standard is applied when evidence supports an unwelcome duty. Believers also avoid assuming every opponent consciously understands truth; Allah knows clarity and motive. They explain accurately and leave full judgment to Him. The settled outcome gives patience without passivity: continue worship and lawful work while temporary debate unfolds. What Allah establishes cannot remain suspended by endless labeling. Desire shifts, but the truth and final account settle every matter.\n\nSettlement also means each disputed claim acquires stability: Muhammad’s truthfulness is established, resurrection occurs, and every deed finds its place. This guards believers from thinking present information chaos is permanent. False claims can dominate a moment because they are repeated quickly; truth may require patient explanation. Allah’s final settlement is not rushed or misinformed. In the meantime, people should settle what lies within their trust through evidence and justice. Do not use the future account as an excuse to leave preventable falsehood unanswered, nor imagine human correction is the final court. Work truthfully and wait faithfully. That patient work trusts the settlement without surrendering present responsibility.",
  "4": "“The stories of destroyed nations that have already come to them” refers to Quranic accounts and known histories of peoples who denied messengers. Noah’s people, 'Ad, Thamud, and others demonstrated that warnings are not empty. Quraysh also passed or knew of ruined settlements.\n\n“A sufficient deterrent” means these reports contain enough to restrain anyone willing to learn. A deterrent works before repetition: one need not personally experience destruction to know the road is dangerous. History becomes mercy when it interrupts the same choice.\n\nThe verse does not authorize calling every archaeological ruin a revealed punishment. It refers to stories Allah has confirmed. Human historical study remains valuable but must distinguish evidence from moral speculation.\n\nReceiving a story is not the same as receiving its lesson. People may enjoy narrative, debate locations, or admire ancient construction while ignoring the warning. The Quran repeatedly asks what happened, why, and what present response differs. Believers should hear the deterrent too; religious identity does not protect arrogance or injustice. A sufficient warning removes the excuse for demanding another spectacle. Wise people learn from another nation’s end while foolish people wait until history becomes their own biography.\n\nA deterrent does not need to reproduce every detail of one’s present case. The nations differed from Quraysh in economy and location, but shared pride and rejection. Moral analogy recognizes the common pattern without pretending histories are identical. This balance matters today. Believers should neither say ancient stories have no relevance nor force every modern society into one prophetic label. Learn stable principles: power is temporary, warning is mercy, injustice carries consequence, and repentance can change direction. Those principles deter across time while specific legal and political applications still require current knowledge and qualified judgment. A living deterrent lets confirmed history reshape conduct before conduct produces another warning history.",
  "5": "“This Quran is profound in wisdom” describes revelation whose instruction reaches goals with precision. Its stories, arguments, warnings, promises, and laws are placed meaningfully. “Profound” also conveys wisdom brought to completion and capable of restraining wrongdoing.\n\nThe Quran’s wisdom appears in connecting the split moon to the Hour, desire to rejection, and history to present accountability. Its warnings are neither random threats nor emotional manipulation. They emerge from perfect knowledge of human choice and destination.\n\n“But warnings are of no benefit to them.” The problem is not deficiency in the warning. Hardened hearers refuse the posture through which benefit enters. A medicine can be effective while someone declines to take it.\n\nThis does not mean reminders never benefit any denier; many opponents later believed. It describes those persisting in the response being addressed. Teachers should not weaken Quranic wisdom by inventing dramatic stories when authentic warning seems insufficient. Convey clearly, embody its ethics, and pray for receptivity. Listeners should avoid pride that says familiar warning has nothing new to teach. Profound wisdom may revisit one truth because the heart needs it in every changed circumstance. Benefit comes when the warning is allowed to restrain, redirect, and awaken.\n\nProfound wisdom can remain useless to someone who approaches only to mock. This reveals a limit of communication, not of content. Teachers can improve clarity but cannot make surrender attractive to a heart committed to pride. They should not respond by diluting truth until it no longer warns. Wisdom chooses tone, timing, and sequence while preserving meaning. Listeners likewise need more than intelligence; they need willingness. A simple verse can benefit a humble person more than an elaborate lecture benefits someone seeking ammunition. The Quran’s depth meets the servant at the point where honest receptivity begins. Benefit is completed when profound wisdom becomes accepted direction and consistent action.",
  "6": "“Turn away from them O Prophet” releases Muhammad from endless dispute with people rejecting every sign. Turning away means disengaging from futile mockery and entrusting the outcome to Allah, not abandoning justice or the wider mission.\n\n“Wait for the Day when the caller will summon them” shifts attention from their present speech to resurrection. The caller, understood as the angel appointed for the blast and gathering, acts by Allah’s command. Everyone who ignored the Messenger will answer this call.\n\n“For something horrifying” describes the unknown event as dreadful and unprecedented. Graves open, people gather, and judgment begins. The exact unseen scene should be affirmed without invented detail.\n\nThe instruction gives emotional balance to callers. They explain, answer sincere questions, and then may leave circular abuse. Ordinary believers do not know a person’s final heart, so turning away remains a boundary rather than a declaration of damnation. They stay open to genuine change. Waiting is active: Muhammad continues worship and teaching while Allah controls timing. A future compulsory summons puts current voluntary listening in perspective. The wise hear Quran now, when response can still be chosen, rather than wait for a call no one can decline.\n\nThe horrifying summons also reverses the pagans’ demand for signs on their schedule. They wanted proof they could evaluate from a position of control. On that Day the proof calls them and controls movement. The difference shows why present revelation is merciful: it speaks without removing choice. Believers should value this quieter invitation. The absence of overwhelming compulsion makes sincere response possible. When reminders seem less dramatic than mockers demand, their very form protects the test. The final caller will be dramatic enough, but the opportunity to earn faith will have ended. Hearing today is the only response that can prepare a person for hearing then.",
  "7": "“With eyes downcast” depicts people emerging in humiliation and fear. Eyes that once looked proudly upon warning cannot meet the terror of judgment with confidence. The posture expresses inward realization, not a command for humans to humiliate anyone.\n\n“They will come forth from the graves” confirms bodily resurrection. Every burial place and every form of lost remains yields its person by Allah’s power. Earth cannot conceal what He commands to emerge.\n\n“As if they were swarming locusts” conveys immense numbers, rapid movement, and apparent disorder under one irresistible direction. Locusts spread across a landscape in masses; humanity will fill the gathering beyond ordinary scale.\n\nThe comparison should not be used to strip resurrected humans of dignity in current life. It communicates movement and multitude at a particular terrifying moment. Each individual within the mass remains fully known and judged by Allah. This gives both warning and comfort: crowds cannot hide wrongdoing, and crowds cannot cause a quiet good to be overlooked. Believers prepare for emergence by lowering pride voluntarily in prayer. A downcast gaze then may be replaced by the honor Allah grants the faithful, while arrogant sight now risks humiliation later.\n\nLocust imagery also communicates how familiar worldly distinctions disappear in the mass movement. Uniform fear overtakes clothing, office, and social rank. Yet divine judgment remains individual. This combination should shape community ethics: people are equal before Allah, but not anonymous. Each right and deed matters. Human systems often do the opposite, honoring rank while losing individuals in crowds. Believers strive to see each person’s dignity now and remember common resurrection. The one ignored in a worldly crowd may have a bright record, while the one elevated above it will still emerge among the swarming multitude. This equal emergence should humble every social rank before the Lord who remembers every individual.",
  "8": "“Rushing towards the caller” continues the locust-like movement. People do not wander or choose another destination. The summons draws them rapidly toward gathering, and every delay once used in worldly life has ended.\n\n“The disbelievers will cry, ‘This is a difficult Day!’” They recognize severity only when it becomes unavoidable. The Day is difficult because their record denied it and contains no faithful preparation. For the righteous, revelation promises protection and ease according to Allah’s mercy.\n\nTheir cry is accurate but late. Earlier prophets described the Day as difficult, yet the same people called warning magic or myth. Information did not benefit until experience forced admission.\n\nThe verse encourages taking trustworthy description seriously before personal sight. People routinely prepare for storms or exams based on reports; the Lord’s warning deserves greater confidence. Believers should not become obsessed with fear alone. They ask Allah for ease, perform good, and hope in mercy. Every response to the prayer call can train prompt movement toward a caller now. Voluntary rushing toward worship is preparation for the Day when movement belongs to command and the meaning of difficulty has already been fixed by one’s choices.\n\nThe difficult Day is also called difficult by those who made warning difficult for themselves. They resisted remembrance, attacked messengers, and followed desire. This does not mean salvation is earned without mercy. It means present choices have real relationship to future experience. Allah can make the Day easy for whom He wills, and believers ask for that favor. Hope grows through faithful preparation rather than empty confidence. A person who finds one duty hard now should compare temporary effort with the difficulty described, seek help, and take manageable consistent steps toward obedience. Hopeful preparation asks Allah to make the unavoidable meeting merciful and easy. That prayer gives urgency a hopeful direction.",
  "9": "“Before them, the people of Noah denied ˹the truth˺ and rejected Our servant, calling ˹him˺ insane. And he was intimidated.” opens the long recital of the destroyed nations that occupies the heart of Surah al-Qamar, and the surah, whose name means “the moon,” turns from the sign of the moon's splitting to the signs of the nations who denied and perished. “Before them, the people of Noah denied the truth and rejected Our servant, calling him insane. And he was intimidated,” or “kaththabat qablahum qawmu Noohin fa-kaththaboo 'abdana wa qaloo majnoonun wazdujir.” Ibn Kathir explains that the people of Noah denied their messenger and called him mad, and the word “wazdujir” — “he was intimidated,” “he was rebuffed,” “he was threatened” — describes the treatment Noah received, for his people not only disbelieved but drove him off with threats and abuse, and the verse tells the Prophet Muhammad, through the memory of Noah, that the messenger before him had been called insane and had been intimidated, and had been answered by his Lord. Al-Tabari notes that the verse is a comfort to the Prophet, for the Quraysh were calling him mad exactly as the people of Noah had called Noah mad, and the surah is telling him that the old accusation is the old enemy and the old answer is the old deliverance.\n\nAl-Razi observes the two descriptions of Noah — “Our servant” and “insane” — placed side by side, and the believer is taught the gap between the judgment of God and the judgment of men, for the one whom God calls His servant the people called insane, and the believer is taught that the worth of a messenger is not decided by the labels of his people but by the station God has given him. The word “'abdana” — “Our servant” — is itself an honor, for the servant of God is the highest title a man can hold, and the believer is taught to desire that title above all others. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to expect the accusation of madness when he carries the truth, for the truth has always been called madness by those whose desires it threatens, and the believer is taught to answer the accusation with the patience of Noah and the trust that his Lord will answer.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to read the story of Noah as the pattern of every prophet's trial, for the calling of insane and the intimidation are the two weapons of the heedless in every age, and the believer is taught to bear them as Noah bore them, and to know that the servant of God who is called insane by men is called servant by the Lord of men.",
  "10": "“So he cried out to his Lord, ‘I am helpless, so help ˹me˺!’” records Noah's prayer in the moment of his extremity, and the verse is one of the Quran's most intimate pictures of the prophet turning to his Lord. “So he cried out to his Lord, 'I am helpless, so help me!'” or “fa-da'a rabbahu annee maghloobun fantasir.” Ibn Kathir explains that Noah, after centuries of patient calling and after being called insane and intimidated by his people, turned at last to his Lord and said, “I am overcome, so help me,” and the word “maghloob” — “overcome,” “vanquished,” “helpless” — is the confession of a prophet who has done everything and now places his cause in the hand of God. Al-Tabari notes that the prayer is the prayer of the one who has exhausted his own strength and found the strength of God, and the believer is taught that there is a moment in every struggle when the servant's strength ends and the Lord's strength begins, and that the recognition of one's helplessness is the beginning of the help.\n\nAl-Razi observes the brevity and the depth of the prayer, for Noah did not ask for a specific punishment but laid his helplessness before his Lord and said, “help me,” and the believer is taught that the best supplication is often the shortest, for the Lord knows the need before it is spoken, and the servant's confession of helplessness is the most eloquent of prayers. The word “fantasir” — “so help,” “so grant me victory” — is the word of one who asks God to take his side, and the believer is taught that the help of God is the only help worth seeking, for the help of men fails and the help of God remains. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to turn to God in the moment of weakness, not after it, and to know that the weakness confessed to God becomes strength, while the weakness hidden from God becomes despair.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to make Noah's prayer his own, to confess his helplessness to the Lord who is never helpless, and to trust that the help asked of God is the help that comes, for the prayer of Noah was answered with the flood and the ark, and the believer who prays as Noah prayed is the believer whose Lord answers as Noah's Lord answered.",
  "11": "“So We opened the gates of the sky with pouring rain,” records the answer to Noah's prayer, and the verse describes the opening of the heavens in language that the commentators unfold with awe. “So We opened the gates of the sky with pouring rain,” or “fa-fatahna abwaba as-sama'i bi-ma'in munhamir.” Ibn Kathir explains that the answer to Noah's prayer was the flood, and the verse describes its coming as the opening of the gates of the sky, for the rain did not fall as ordinary rain but poured down as if the heavens had been opened and their waters released, and the word “munhamir” — “pouring,” “streaming” — describes the torrential abundance of the water. Al-Tabari notes that the verse teaches the believer the power of God over the heavens, for the sky which holds back its water by the command of God releases it by the same command, and the gates which open for the flood are the gates which open for the mercy, and the believer is taught that the heavens obey their Lord.\n\nAl-Razi observes the fittingness of the answer to the prayer, for Noah had been called insane and had been rebuffed, and the answer came not in words but in water, and the believer is taught that the help of God is not always the help that men expect, for the help that came to Noah came in the form of a flood that drowned his enemies and carried his ark, and the believer is taught to trust the wisdom of the form in which the help arrives. The verse also teaches the believer the reality of the punishment, for the flood was not a metaphor but a drowning, and the believer is taught that the punishment of God is real and that the warning of it is mercy. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to fear the water of the sky as one of the armies of God, for the same rain that gives life to the earth was made the instrument of the flood, and the believer is taught to know that the elements are servants of their Lord, sent as He wills, for mercy or for judgment.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to see the hand of God in the ordinary forces of nature, for the rain that falls gently on his fields is the same water that drowned the people of Noah, and the believer is taught to thank God for the mercy of the ordinary and to fear the judgment that the same elements can carry.",
  "12": "“and caused the earth to burst with springs, so the waters met for a fate already set.” completes the description of the flood by joining the water of the sky to the water of the earth, and the verse shows the two waters meeting to fulfill the decree. “And caused the earth to burst with springs, so the waters met for a fate already set,” or “wa fajjarna al-arda 'uyoonan faltaqa al-ma'u 'ala amrin qad qudir.” Ibn Kathir explains that the earth was made to burst forth with springs, so that the water rose from beneath as it fell from above, and the two waters met — the water of the sky and the water of the earth — for a purpose that had been decreed, and the believer is taught that the flood was not an accident of nature but a decree of God, planned and measured before it came. Al-Tabari notes the word “qudir” — “was decreed,” “was measured” — and explains that the meeting of the waters was for a fate that God had already set, so that the flood came at its appointed time and to its appointed measure, and the believer is taught that the decrees of God are precise and sure.\n\nAl-Razi observes the lesson of the two waters, for the punishment came from above and from below at once, so that the people of Noah were surrounded by the water of the sky and the water of the earth, and there was no direction of escape, and the believer is taught that the judgment of God, when it comes, comes from every side, and that the one who has no refuge with God has no refuge at all. The verse also teaches the believer the completeness of the divine power, for the same Lord who opened the gates of the sky opened the springs of the earth, and the believer is taught that the Lord of the heavens and the earth commands both with a single word. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to see the natural world as the instrument of the divine will, for the springs of the earth are not dead matter but servants of their Lord, and the believer is taught to fear the Lord of the springs as he hopes in the Lord of the rain.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to trust the precision of the decree, for the waters met “for a fate already set,” and the believer is taught that the events of his own life are not random but measured, and that the believer who trusts the decree meets the events with the serenity of one who knows that the Lord has set them.",
  "13": "“We carried him on that ˹Ark made˺ of planks and nails,” records the means of Noah's deliverance, and the verse turns from the waters of the punishment to the vessel of the mercy. “We carried him on that Ark made of planks and nails,” or “wa hamalnahu 'ala thati alwahin wa dusur.” Ibn Kathir explains that the ark was built of planks and fastened with nails, and the believer is taught that the ark of Noah, for all its plain materials, was the vessel of the greatest deliverance, and that the planks and nails of a boat were made the means by which God saved His servant and the believers with him. Al-Tabari notes the detail of the materials, for the Quran names the planks and the nails to show that the ark was not a marvel of construction but an ordinary boat built by human hands, and the believer is taught that the deliverance of God does not depend on the splendor of the means but on the command of God, and that the plainest vessel, when God wills, becomes the ark of salvation.\n\nAl-Razi observes the lesson of the ark, for the ark was built over a long time, under the mockery of Noah's people, and then became the only refuge when the flood came, and the believer is taught that the work done in obedience to God, though mocked by men, is the work that saves, and that the mockery of the heedless does not diminish the value of the work in the sight of God. The verse also teaches the believer the combination of human effort and divine help, for Noah built the ark and God carried it, and the believer is taught to do his part and to trust God for the rest, for the deliverance of God comes through the means that the believer prepares and the command that God gives. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to prepare for the floods of life with the ark of obedience, and to know that the ark which carries him is the ark of faith and good work, built plank by plank through the years.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to see his own deeds as the planks of his ark, for the believer who builds in obedience is carried by God when the waters rise, and the believer is taught to build while there is time, for the flood comes upon those who did not build and the ark carries those who did.",
  "14": "“sailing under Our ˹watchful˺ Eyes—a ˹fair˺ punishment on behalf of the one ˹they˺ denied.” describes the ark's voyage and the purpose of the flood, and the verse gathers the mercy and the judgment into a single statement. “Sailing under Our watchful Eyes — a fair punishment on behalf of the one they denied,” or “tajree bi-a'yunina jaza'an li-man kana kufir.” Ibn Kathir explains that the ark sailed under the eyes of God, watched and guarded and guided by Him, and the phrase “bi-a'yunina” — “under Our eyes” — is the Quran's way of saying that the ark and its passengers were under the direct care of God, protected from the flood that destroyed everything around them. Al-Tabari notes that the ark's sailing was not the sailing of a boat left to the waves but the sailing of a vessel under the eye of its Lord, and the believer is taught that the servant who is under the eye of God is safe in the midst of the flood.\n\nThe second half of the verse states the double purpose: “a fair punishment on behalf of the one they denied,” or “jaza'an li-man kana kufir.” Al-Razi explains that the flood was a recompense, a requital, for Noah, the one whom they had denied, so that the same event that drowned the deniers was a vindication of the one they denied, and the believer is taught that the judgment of God is always a requital, the reward of the patient messenger and the punishment of the heedless people in a single decree. The word “kufir” — “was denied” — is the passive form, and the believer is taught that the denial of the messenger is the denial that the punishment requites. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to trust the justice of God, for the flood that seemed to be mere destruction was a requital that honored the one denied and punished the deniers, and the believer is taught to know that his own faithfulness will be requited and the denial of his message weighed.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to place himself under the eye of God, for the one who is watched by God is guarded by God, and the believer is taught to know that the flood of judgment is a requital, not a randomness, and that the believer who is faithful will be carried as Noah was carried, while the deniers sink as Noah's people sank.",
  "15": "“We certainly left this as a sign. So is there anyone who will be mindful?” states the enduring purpose of the flood, and the verse turns the event of the past into a sign for the future. “We certainly left this as a sign. So is there anyone who will be mindful?” or “wa laqad taraknaha ayatan fa-hal min muddakir.” Ibn Kathir explains that God left the flood, or the ark, or the story itself, as a sign that remains, and the believer is taught that the events of the past are not erased but preserved as signs, so that every later generation may learn from them without having to live through them. Al-Tabari notes that the sign may refer to the ark, which the commentators say remained on Mount Judi as a witness, or to the story preserved in revelation, and in either case the point is the same — the flood was left as a lesson, and the lesson remains. The question “is there anyone who will be mindful?” — “fa-hal min muddakir” — is the Quran's summons to reflection, and the believer is taught that the sign profits only the one who reflects upon it.\n\nAl-Razi observes the word “muddakir” — “one who remembers,” “one who takes heed” — which comes from the root of remembrance, and the believer is taught that the sign of the flood is a reminder, and that the remembrance it calls for is not a mere recollection of a story but the taking of the lesson into the heart and the life. The question is asked in a form that leaves the answer to the hearer, and the believer is taught to answer it with his own reflection, for the one who hears the sign and does not reflect has not heard it. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to read the signs of the past with the eye of the present, for the flood is not a story about the dead but a warning to the living, and the believer is taught to be among the “muddakirin” — those who take heed — and not among those who pass over the sign.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to answer the question of the verse with his own mindfulness, for the sign of the flood remains and the question remains, and the believer is taught to let the sign do its work in his heart, and to be one of the few who, when asked “is there anyone who will be mindful,” answers with a heart that has taken heed.",
  "16": "“Then how ˹dreadful˺ were My punishment and warnings!” introduces the refrain of the surah, and the verse gives voice to the severity of the judgment in the words of the Judge Himself. “Then how dreadful were My punishment and warnings!” or “fa-kayfa kana 'athabee wa nuthur.” Ibn Kathir explains that the verse is the question of God to the hearer, asking how the punishment and the warnings of God were — and the implied answer is that they were dreadful beyond description, for the flood that drowned the people of Noah was a punishment and a warning whose severity no tongue could tell. Al-Tabari notes that the verse is spoken by God in the first person — “My punishment and My warnings” — and the believer is taught that the severity of the judgment is not a matter of human report but of divine declaration, and that the One who warns is the One who punished, and the warning is therefore as sure as the punishment.\n\nAl-Razi observes the structure of the refrain, for the surah repeats it after each nation — Noah, 'Ad, Thamud, Lot, and Pharaoh — and the repetition teaches the believer that the punishment and the warning are one, and that the warning which preceded each punishment was as severe as the punishment itself, for the warning is the mercy that holds the punishment back while the heart can still turn. The word “nuthur” — “warnings” — is plural, and the believer is taught that the warnings of God are many, repeated and varied, so that no one can say the warning was not given, and the believer is taught to count the warnings he has received as the mercies they are. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to fear the punishment through the warning, for the one who fears the warning is spared the punishment, and the believer is taught to hear the question “how dreadful were My punishment and warnings” as the question that is answered by his own turning.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to tremble at the question and to let the trembling turn him, for the punishment of God is dreadful and the warning of God is mercy, and the believer is taught to take the warning before the punishment, and to know that the question of the verse is asked so that the believer may never have to ask it of himself.",
  "17": "“And We have certainly made the Quran easy to remember. So is there anyone who will be mindful?” introduces the second refrain of the surah, and the verse places the ease of the Quran beside the severity of the punishment, so that the hearer stands between the two. “And We have certainly made the Quran easy to remember. So is there anyone who will be mindful?” or “wa laqad yassarna al-Qur'ana lil-dhikri fa-hal min muddakir.” Ibn Kathir explains that God has made the Quran easy for remembrance — easy to recite, easy to memorize, easy to understand and to take to heart — and the ease of the Quran is one of the great mercies of God, for the guidance of the Quran is made available to every seeker, the learned and the simple, the memorizer and the listener. Al-Tabari notes that the verse is repeated four times in the surah, after the account of each nation, and the repetition teaches the believer that the Quran is the refuge from the fates that the surah has been reciting, for the one who takes the Quran to heart is spared the punishment that overtook the heedless.\n\nAl-Razi observes the word “yassarna” — “We have made easy” — and explains that the ease of the Quran is itself a sign, for a book of such depth and such guidance could have been made difficult, and its ease is the proof that God desires its remembrance and its following, and the believer is taught to thank God for the ease and to use it, for the ease that is not used is a mercy that is refused. The question “is there anyone who will be mindful” links the two refrains, for the mindfulness that the punishment warns toward is the mindfulness that the Quran invites toward, and the believer is taught that the Quran is the means of the mindfulness that saves. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to make the Quran his companion, for the Quran is made easy for the very purpose of remembrance, and the believer who turns away from the easy has no excuse, for the path was made plain and the door was left open.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to answer the question of the verse with the action of remembrance, for the Quran is easy and the mindfulness is possible, and the believer is taught to take the Quran as the rope that God has lowered, and to climb it out of the fates that the surah has recited.",
  "18": "“’Ȃd ˹also˺ rejected ˹the truth˺. Then how ˹dreadful˺ were My punishment and warnings!” turns to the second nation of the recital, and the surah moves from Noah to 'Ad with the same refrain closing each account. “'Ad also rejected the truth. Then how dreadful were My punishment and warnings!” or “kaththabat 'Adun fa-kayfa kana 'athabee wa nuthur.” Ibn Kathir explains that 'Ad, the people of Hud, rejected the truth that their messenger brought, and the verse then asks the same question of the severity of the punishment and the warnings, for the punishment of 'Ad was the shrieking wind that raged for seven nights and eight days, and the warnings of Hud had preceded it with every argument and every counsel. Al-Tabari notes that the surah does not retell the story of 'Ad at length here but names them and asks the question, for the story is known from its fuller tellings elsewhere, and the surah's method is the method of the refrain — to name and to ask, so that the hearer supplies the story from memory and feels the weight of the question.\n\nAl-Razi observes the purpose of the brevity, for the surah is a surah of refrains, and the refrains are the skeleton on which the recital hangs, and the believer is taught to read the surah as a whole, letting the repeated questions build their weight in the heart, and to know that the nations named are not being introduced but invoked, each name a door into a story already known. The verse also teaches the believer the single fact that matters about each nation — that they rejected — and the believer is taught that the rejection of the truth is the one sin that the surah records of them all, and that the rejection is the cause and the punishment is the effect, joined by the question that asks how dreadful the effect was. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to hear the name of each nation as the name of a possible fate, and to ask the question of the verse of his own possible end.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to let the refrain do its work, for the question “how dreadful were My punishment and warnings” is the question that turns the hearer from the story of 'Ad to the state of his own heart, and the believer is taught to answer the question with the fear that leads to safety.",
  "19": "“Indeed, We sent against them a furious wind, on a day of unrelenting misery,” describes the punishment of 'Ad, and the verse gives the image of the wind that the surah's question had pointed toward. “Indeed, We sent against them a furious wind, on a day of unrelenting misery,” or “inna arsalna 'alayhim reehan sarsaran fee yawmi nahsin mustamirr.” Ibn Kathir explains that the wind sent against 'Ad was a “sarsar” — a violently cold, shrieking wind — and the day was a day of “nahs” — of misery, of calamity — and the word “mustamirr” — “unrelenting,” “continuous” — describes the misery that did not ease, for the wind blew for seven nights and eight days without pause, and the believer is taught that the punishment of 'Ad was not a moment of terror but a span of it, an unbroken misery that ground them down. Al-Tabari notes the precision of the description, for the verse names the nature of the wind and the nature of the day, and the believer is taught that the punishment of God is measured and described in the Quran so that the warning may be vivid.\n\nAl-Razi observes the fittingness of the punishment, for 'Ad had boasted of their strength and their bodies, and the punishment came in the form of a wind, the most bodiless of forces, so that the strongest of peoples was undone by the thing they could not seize, and the believer is taught that the power of God is not measured by the power of men, and that the one who trusts in his strength trusts in what a wind can scatter. The word “mustamirr” also teaches the believer the nature of the punishment of God, for the punishment of God is not a passing storm but an unrelenting decree, and the believer is taught to fear the punishment that does not relent, and to seek the mercy that does. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to see the wind itself as a sign, for the wind that cools and carries is the same wind that destroyed 'Ad, and the believer is taught to fear the Lord of the wind and to thank Him for the mercy of the wind he breathes.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to read the description of the wind with awe, for the wind of 'Ad is a warning that the ordinary forces of the world are the armies of God, and the believer is taught to live in the fear of the One who commands them.",
  "20": "“that snatched people up, leaving them like trunks of uprooted palm trees.” completes the image of 'Ad's destruction, and the verse gives the comparison by which the Quran makes the ruin visible. “That snatched people up, leaving them like trunks of uprooted palm trees,” or “tanzi'u an-nasa ka-annahum a'jazu nakhlin munqa'ir.” Ibn Kathir explains that the wind snatched the people up and cast them down, so that they lay like the trunks of palm trees torn from the ground, and the commentators dwell on the image, for the people of 'Ad were a people of great stature and strength, and the verse compares them to uprooted palm trunks, mighty in size but thrown down and emptied of life, so that the very strength that had made them boast became the image of their ruin. Al-Tabari notes the word “munqa'ir” — “uprooted,” “torn out” — for the palm trunk is the image of the thing torn from its place and cast aside, and the believer is taught that the strong who are uprooted from their trust in God are like the palm torn from its root.\n\nAl-Razi observes the precision of the comparison, for the palm tree is the symbol of the land of 'Ad, the tree of their valleys and their dates, and the comparison of the people to the trunks of their own palms makes the ruin vivid to the hearer, and the believer is taught that the punishment of God is described in the imagery of the punished, so that the warning reaches the heart through the familiar. The verse also teaches the believer the fate of strength that denies its source, for the palm stands by its root, and the man stands by his Lord, and the one who is torn from his Lord is torn up as the palm is torn up, and the believer is taught to deepen his root in God so that no wind may uproot him. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to read the image of the uprooted palms as the image of every life torn from its source, and to cling to the root of faith, for the wind of judgment uproots only those whose roots were not in God.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to let the image of 'Ad remain before his eyes, for the uprooted palms are the end of the boastful in every age, and the believer is taught to seek the rootedness that no wind can undo, the rootedness of the heart in the Lord of the wind.",
  "21": "“Then how ˹dreadful˺ were My punishment and warnings!” closes the account of 'Ad with the refrain, and the verse repeats the question that seals each nation's story. “Then how dreadful were My punishment and warnings!” or “fa-kayfa kana 'athabee wa nuthur.” Ibn Kathir explains that the question is asked of the hearer, and the implied answer — that the punishment and the warnings were dreadful beyond measure — is the lesson that the refrain fixes in the heart. Al-Tabari notes that the repetition of the refrain is not a mere literary device but a deliberate summoning, for the question is asked after each nation so that the hearer may pause and measure what he has heard, and the believer is taught to pause at each refrain and to let the question weigh upon him. The punishment of 'Ad is now complete in the recital — the wind, the day of misery, the uprooted palms — and the question gathers it all into the single point that the surah is making.\n\nAl-Razi observes the function of the refrain in the structure of the surah, for the surah is built of accounts and refrains, and the refrain is the hammer that drives the account home, and the believer is taught to read the surah with the rhythm of its refrains, letting each account end in the question that turns it toward him. The verse also teaches the believer the unity of the punishment and the warning, for the question joins them — “My punishment and My warnings” — and the believer is taught that the warning is the mercy that precedes the punishment, and that the one who receives the warning is spared the punishment, and the one who refuses the warning receives the punishment that the question asks about. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to answer the question of the refrain with the turning of his heart, for the question is not asked for information but for transformation.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to let the refrains of the surah become the refrains of his own reflection, for the question “how dreadful were My punishment and warnings” is the question that the believer should ask himself after every account of the destroyed nations, and the believer is taught to answer it with the fear that guards him from the fate it describes.",
  "22": "“And We have certainly made the Quran easy to remember. So is there anyone who will be mindful?” follows the punishment of 'Ad with the refrain of the Quran's ease, and the verse places the remedy beside the disease. “And We have certainly made the Quran easy to remember. So is there anyone who will be mindful?” or “wa laqad yassarna al-Qur'ana lil-dhikri fa-hal min muddakir.” Ibn Kathir explains that the ease of the Quran is asserted again after the account of 'Ad, and the believer is taught that the Quran is the means by which the fate of 'Ad may be avoided, for the one who takes the Quran to heart is the one who remembers and is spared. Al-Tabari notes that the repetition of the refrain of ease after each account of punishment is the surah's way of saying that the warning and the remedy are given together, and that no one who hears the warning is left without the means of escape.\n\nAl-Razi observes the pairing of the two refrains, for the question of the punishment and the question of the mindfulness are the two hands of the one call, and the believer is taught to hear them together — the dread of the punishment driving him toward the ease of the Quran, and the ease of the Quran sheltering him from the dread of the punishment. The word “lil-dhikri” — “for remembrance” — is the key, for the Quran is made easy not for idle reading but for remembrance, and the believer is taught that the purpose of the Quran's ease is the remembrance of God, and that the remembrance is the safety from the fates that the surah has recited. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to use the ease of the Quran, for the ease that is given is a trust, and the believer who does not take the Quran to heart has refused the remedy that was made easy for him, and has no excuse for the refusal.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to answer the question of the ease with the act of remembrance, for the Quran is easy and the mindfulness is within reach, and the believer is taught to make the Quran his daily companion, so that the ease of the Quran becomes the ease of his path, and the remembrance becomes the shield against the punishment that the surah describes.",
  "23": "“Thamûd rejected the warnings ˹as well˺,” turns to the third nation of the recital, and the surah moves from 'Ad to Thamud with the same opening that named the others. “Thamud rejected the warnings as well,” or “kaththabat Thamoodu bin-nuthur.” Ibn Kathir explains that Thamud, the people of Salih, rejected the warnings that their messenger brought, and the word “nuthur” — “warnings” — is the word that the surah has been using for the message itself, for the message of the prophets is a warning before it is anything else, and the believer is taught that the rejection of the warning is the rejection of the whole message, for the one who refuses to be warned refuses to be saved. Al-Tabari notes that the verse opens the account of Thamud with the same word that opened the account of the others, and the believer is taught that the nations differed in their lands and their times but were one in their response — the rejection of the warning — and that the one response is the one cause of their ruin.\n\nAl-Razi observes the brevity of the opening, for the surah does not yet describe Thamud's sin or their punishment but names their rejection first, and the believer is taught that the rejection is the root, and the sin and the punishment are its branches, and the believer is taught to guard the root, for the one who keeps the root of acceptance keeps the tree of safety. The verse also prepares the ground for the account of Thamud's argument that follows, for the surah is about to record the reasoning by which Thamud rejected, and the believer is taught to hear their reasoning as the reasoning of the heedless in every age, and to recognize it when it is repeated. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to receive the warnings of God as warnings, to let them warn him, and to know that the rejection of the warning is the rejection that the surah records of nation after nation, and that its end is the end that the surah records.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to be among those who accept the warnings, for the warning is the mercy that God sends before the judgment, and the believer is taught to answer the warning with acceptance, and to know that the acceptance of the warning is the first step on the path of the saved.",
  "24": "“arguing, ‘How can we follow one ˹average˺ human being from among us? We would then truly be misguided and insane.” records the argument by which Thamud rejected their messenger, and the verse exposes the logic of the heedless in their own words. “Arguing, 'How can we follow one average human being from among us? We would then truly be misguided and insane,'” or “fa-qaloo a-basharan minna wahidan nattabi'uhu inna ithan la-fee dalalin wa su'ur.” Ibn Kathir explains that Thamud objected to following a single human being from among themselves, and the objection is the ancient objection of the heedless, for they measured the truth by the number and the rank of its bearer, and they concluded that following one ordinary man would be misguidance and madness, and the believer is taught that the objection is the exact reverse of the truth, for the misguidance lay in refusing the messenger, not in following him. Al-Tabari notes that the argument was not an argument at all but a declaration of pride, for the people of Thamud could not accept that the truth should come through one of their own, and the believer is taught that the truth is not diminished by the humbleness of its bearer.\n\nAl-Razi observes the two parts of their reasoning — “one” and “from among us” — and explains that they objected to the singularity of the messenger and to his ordinariness, and the believer is taught that both objections are answered by the wisdom of God, for the messenger is one because the message is one, and the messenger is from among the people because the message must be lived among them, and the believer is taught to receive the messenger as God sends him, not as the people would choose him. The phrase “we would then truly be misguided and insane” is the irony that the verse preserves, for the people of Thamud called the guidance misguidance and the truth madness, and the believer is taught that the heedless reverse the names of things, and that the believer must not let their reversal confuse him. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to follow the truth wherever it comes from, for the truth is true whoever bears it, and the believer is taught to know that the objection of Thamud is the objection that the Quraysh made to the Prophet, and that its end is the end that the surah records.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to recognize the voice of Thamud in the objections of his own age, for the demand that the truth come with a retinue and a rank is the old demand, and the believer is taught to answer it with the certainty that the truth needs no retinue, and that the madness is in the refusal, not in the following.",
  "25": "“Has the revelation been sent down ˹only˺ to him out of ˹all of˺ us? In fact, he is a boastful liar.’” completes the argument of Thamud, and the verse records the final accusation by which they dismissed their messenger. “Has the revelation been sent down only to him out of all of us? In fact, he is a boastful liar,” or “a-ulqiya ath-thikru 'alayhi min baynina bal huwa kaththabun ashir.” Ibn Kathir explains that Thamud asked, in effect, why the revelation should have been sent to Salih alone out of all of them, and then answered their own question with the accusation that he was a boastful liar, and the believer is taught that the two moves — the question and the accusation — are the two moves of every people who rejected their messenger, for the question expresses their envy of the one chosen and the accusation expresses their refusal of the one who warned. Al-Tabari notes the word “ath-thikr” — “the reminder,” “the revelation” — for the message is the reminder, and the people of Thamud resented that the reminder should have come to one of them, and the believer is taught that the choice of the messenger is God's, and that the resentment of the choice is the resentment of the Chooser.\n\nAl-Razi observes the irony of the accusation, for the people of Thamud called Salih a liar and a boaster, when it was they who were false in their refusal and boastful in their pride, and the believer is taught that the heedless accuse the truthful of their own faults, and that the believer must not be shaken by the accusation, for the accusation is the reflection of the accuser. The word “ashir” — “boastful,” “arrogant” — is the word that the verse turns back upon them, for the boaster is not the one who warns but the one who refuses the warning out of pride, and the believer is taught to guard against the boastfulness that refuses the truth because of who bears it. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to accept the choice of God in the matter of His messengers, for the choice is the wisdom of God, and the believer is taught to know that the accusation of the messenger is the accusation of the Sender, and that its end is the end that the surah records.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to see his own possible objections in the words of Thamud, for the heart that asks why the truth came through this one and not through me is the heart that is already refusing, and the believer is taught to silence that heart with the remembrance that the choice is God's, and the following is the servant's.",
  "26": "“˹It was revealed to Ṣâliḥ,˺ ‘They will soon know who the boastful liar is.” records the answer that God gave to the accusation of Thamud, and the verse turns their slander back upon them with a warning of certainty. “It was revealed to Salih, 'They will soon know who the boastful liar is,'” or “sa-ya'lamoona ghadan mani al-kaththabu al-ashir.” Ibn Kathir explains that God revealed to Salih the answer to the accusation, and the answer was not an argument but a warning — they would soon know, on the morrow, who was the boastful liar, Salih or themselves — and the believer is taught that the truth does not need to defend itself against slander, for time and the decree of God will reveal who was truthful and who was false. Al-Tabari notes the word “ghadan” — “tomorrow,” “soon” — for the answer pointed to the nearness of the judgment, and the believer is taught that the vindication of the truthful is near, and that the slander of the heedless is short-lived.\n\nAl-Razi observes the wisdom of the answer, for the accusation of Thamud was an accusation about character — “boastful liar” — and the answer of God was a warning about the near future, and the believer is taught that the best answer to the slander of the heedless is not the defense of the self but the patience that waits for the judgment, for the judgment will show who was the liar. The verse also teaches the believer the certainty of the divine word, for the warning “they will soon know” is the word of God, and the word of God cannot fail, and the believer is taught to trust the word of God over the word of men, and to wait with patience for the fulfillment. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to leave his vindication to God, for the one who defends himself too eagerly has forgotten that God is his defender, and the believer is taught to let the truth speak for itself and to let God judge between him and his slanderers.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught the patience of Salih, for the messenger who was called a boastful liar did not rage but waited, and the believer is taught to wait as Salih waited, and to know that the morrow of the truthful is the day of their vindication, while the morrow of the slanderer is the day of their exposure.",
  "27": "“We are sending the she-camel as a test for them. So watch them ˹closely˺, and have patience.” records the test that God sent to Thamud, and the verse introduces the she-camel that became the sign of their fate. “We are sending the she-camel as a test for them. So watch them closely, and have patience,” or “inna mursiloo an-naqati fitnatan lahum far-taqibhum wastabir.” Ibn Kathir explains that the she-camel was sent as a test — “fitnah” — for the people of Thamud, a sign that would prove Salih's truth and a trial that would test their obedience, for the she-camel came forth from the rock before their eyes, and they were commanded to respect her and to share the water with her, and the believer is taught that the signs of God are always tests, for the sign proves the truth and the response to the sign reveals the heart. Al-Tabari notes that God addressed Salih with the command to watch and to be patient, for the test of the she-camel would unfold over days, and the believer is taught that the messenger who delivers the sign must wait upon its outcome with patience.\n\nAl-Razi observes the word “fitnah” — “test,” “trial” — and explains that the she-camel was a trial for Thamud in the fullest sense, for it placed before them a clear proof and a clear command, and their treatment of it would decide their fate, and the believer is taught that the trials of God are not arbitrary but are the occasions of decision, in which the heart declares itself for the truth or against it. The command “watch them closely, and have patience” is the command to the messenger to observe the outcome without forcing it, and the believer is taught that the one who delivers the truth must let the truth work, and that the patience of the messenger is the patience of the one who knows that the judgment is God's. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to see the signs of God as tests, to receive them with the seriousness of one who is being tried, and to know that the test of the she-camel is the test of every sign, in which the response reveals the responder.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to watch and to be patient, for the truth is not established in a day, and the believer is taught to deliver the sign and to wait upon the Lord, and to know that the sign which is a test will be a witness, for or against, on the day of the outcome.",
  "28": "“And tell them that the ˹drinking˺ water must be divided between them ˹and her˺, each taking a turn to drink ˹every other day˺.’” states the command that accompanied the she-camel, and the verse gives the detail of the test by which Thamud would be tried. “And tell them that the drinking water must be divided between them and her, each taking a turn to drink every other day,” or “wa nabbi'hum anna al-ma'a qismatun baynahum kullu shirbin muhtadar.” Ibn Kathir explains that the water of the well was to be divided between the people of Thamud and the she-camel, each taking a turn on alternate days — the camel drinking on one day and the people on the next — and the believer is taught that the test was a test of justice and obedience, for the command required them to share the water with a creature they had not wanted and to respect the division that God had set. Al-Tabari notes the word “muhtadar” — “attended,” “by turns” — for the turns were fixed and known, and the believer is taught that the command of God comes with its details, and that obedience is in the details no less than in the principles.\n\nAl-Razi observes the wisdom of the test, for the command touched the most vital of their resources — the water of the desert — and the believer is taught that the trials of God often touch the very things the heart clings to, and that the response to the trial reveals whether the heart clings to God or to the thing. The sharing of the water was a small command in itself, but its meaning was large, for it was the acknowledgment that the sign of God has rights, and the believer is taught that the one who respects the small commands of God is the one who respects the great, and the one who begrudges the small is the one who will refuse the great. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to obey God in the details, for the command that seems small is the test of the obedience that is great, and the believer is taught to share what God has commanded him to share, and to know that the division set by God is the division of justice.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to see the justice of God in the division of the water, for the command gave the she-camel her turn and the people theirs, and the believer is taught to give every right its due, and to know that the one who respects the rights that God has set is the one who passes the test.",
  "29": "“But they roused a companion of theirs, so he dared to kill ˹her˺.” records the act by which Thamud failed the test, and the verse describes the killing of the she-camel in its ugliest detail. “But they roused a companion of theirs, so he dared to kill her,” or “fa-nadaw sahibahum fa-ta'ata fa-'aqar.” Ibn Kathir explains that the people of Thamud called upon one of their companions — the most wretched of them, as the Quran elsewhere names him — and he went forward and hamstrung and killed the she-camel, and the believer is taught that the sin of Thamud was a collective sin, for the whole people roused the one who killed, and the one who killed was the hand of the many who consented. Al-Tabari notes the word “fa-ta'ata” — “he dared,” “he undertook” — for the killer acted boldly, in defiance, and the believer is taught that the boldness of sin is the mark of the heart that has hardened, and that the hardening is the fruit of the long refusal that preceded the act.\n\nAl-Razi observes the lesson of the collective guilt, for the verse does not say that one man killed the she-camel but that the people roused a companion of theirs, and the believer is taught that the sin which is consented to is shared, and that the community which approves the wrong shares the guilt of the one who commits it, and the believer is taught to withhold his consent from wrong, for the consent is the participation. The killing of the she-camel was the crossing of the final line, the act by which Thamud declared war on the sign of God, and the believer is taught that every test has its final line, and that the one who crosses it has chosen the judgment. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to guard against being the companion who is roused to sin, for the sin that is done at the call of others is still the sin of the one who did it, and the believer is taught to refuse the call to wrong, and to be among those who withhold their hands and their consent.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to see the killing of the she-camel as the image of every defiance of the signs of God, for the sign of God is sacred, and the one who violates the sacred thing violates the Sender of the sign, and the believer is taught to honor the signs of God in whatever form they come, and to know that the violation of the sign is the invocation of the judgment.",
  "30": "“Then how ˹dreadful˺ were My punishment and warnings!” follows the killing of the she-camel with the refrain, and the verse places the judgment immediately upon the crime. “Then how dreadful were My punishment and warnings!” or “fa-kayfa kana 'athabee wa nuthur.” Ibn Kathir explains that the punishment of Thamud followed their crime, and the question of the refrain asks the hearer to consider how dreadful that punishment was — the blast that overtook them and left them like the twigs of a fence, as the next verse describes — and the believer is taught that the refrain is placed here, immediately after the killing, to show that the judgment follows the sin without delay and that the warning which preceded it was as severe as the punishment that fulfilled it. Al-Tabari notes that the refrain is the surah's way of joining the crime and the consequence in a single breath, and the believer is taught to read the crime and the consequence together.\n\nAl-Razi observes the order of the verses, for the surah records the sin and then the refrain and then the description of the punishment, and the believer is taught that the question of the refrain stands between the sin and its description as the pause in which the hearer is meant to measure what he has heard, and the believer is taught to pause at the refrain and to let the question do its work. The verse also teaches the believer the severity of the warning, for the people of Thamud had been warned through Salih and through the she-camel and through the division of the water, and the warning had been as severe as the punishment, and the believer is taught to measure the mercy of the warning against the dread of the punishment, and to choose the warning while the choice remains. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to hear the refrain as the voice of the Lord asking him, and to answer the question with the fear that turns.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to let the refrain fall upon his heart after each account, for the question of the Lord is not rhetorical but real, and the believer is taught to answer it with the turning that the question intends.",
  "31": "“Indeed, We sent against them ˹only˺ one ˹mighty˺ blast, leaving them like the twigs of fence-builders.” describes the punishment of Thamud, and the verse gives the image by which their end is remembered. “Indeed, We sent against them only one mighty blast, leaving them like the twigs of fence-builders,” or “inna arsalna 'alayhim sayhatan wahidatan fa-kanu ka-hasheemi al-muhtathir.” Ibn Kathir explains that the punishment of Thamud was a single blast — one overwhelming sound, one cry from the sky — and the word “wahidah” — “one” — is emphatic, for a single blast was enough to destroy a whole people, and the believer is taught that the punishment of God needs no repetition and no army, for one command, one sound, one blast is enough to undo the mighty. Al-Tabari notes the image of the “hasheem al-muhtathir” — the dry twigs and thorns that a fence-builder gathers and scatters — for the people of Thamud, who had carved their dwellings out of the rock, were reduced to the likeness of dry, broken twigs, scattered and lifeless, and the believer is taught that the strength of the strong is, before the command of God, no more than the dry twig before the flame.\n\nAl-Razi observes the fittingness of the image, for the people of Thamud had built in stone and had trusted in their stone, and the verse compares them to the most fragile of things — the dry twigs of the desert — so that the contrast between their trust and their end is the lesson, and the believer is taught that the one who trusts in stone will be scattered like straw, while the one who trusts in God will stand. The verse also teaches the believer the swiftness of the divine punishment, for the blast was one and the destruction was complete, and the believer is taught that the judgment of God, when it comes, comes in an instant, and that the preparation for it must not be delayed. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to read the image of the dry twigs as the image of every worldly trust, for the world and its strength are the stuff of twigs, and the believer is taught to build upon the rock of faith, which no blast can scatter.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to fear the blast that comes in an instant, and to know that the one blast that destroyed Thamud is the proof that the judgment of God is not slow, and the believer is taught to prepare for the instant with the work of a lifetime, so that when the instant comes, it finds him among the saved.",
  "32": "“And We have certainly made the Quran easy to remember. So is there anyone who will be mindful?” follows the punishment of Thamud with the refrain of the Quran's ease, and the verse repeats the remedy after the warning. “And We have certainly made the Quran easy to remember. So is there anyone who will be mindful?” or “wa laqad yassarna al-Qur'ana lil-dhikri fa-hal min muddakir.” Ibn Kathir explains that the ease of the Quran is asserted again after the account of Thamud, and the believer is taught that the Quran is the means by which the fate of Thamud may be avoided, for the one who takes the Quran to heart remembers and is saved. Al-Tabari notes that the refrain is repeated after each nation, and the believer is taught that the repetition is the mercy of God, for the reminder is given again and again, so that no one may say the reminder was not given, and the believer is taught to receive each repetition as a fresh opportunity.\n\nAl-Razi observes the pairing of the refrains once more, for the dread of the blast and the ease of the Quran are set side by side, and the believer is taught that the Quran is the refuge from the blast, and that the one who holds the Quran holds the rope that lifts him from the fates that the surah recites. The word “lil-dhikri” — “for remembrance” — is the purpose of the ease, and the believer is taught that the Quran is not made easy for entertainment but for remembrance, and that the remembrance is the shield against the blast, for the one who remembers God is remembered by God. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to answer the question of the refrain with the action of the heart, for the mindfulness that the question asks for is not a passing thought but a turning, and the believer is taught to turn to the Quran and to let its ease become his ease.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to hear the refrain as the voice of mercy, for the same Lord who sends the blast has made the Quran easy, and the believer is taught to take the mercy while it is offered, and to know that the ease of the Quran is the proof that God desires the salvation of His servants, and the believer is taught to desire it for himself.",
  "33": "“The people of Lot ˹also˺ rejected the warnings.” turns to the fourth nation of the recital, and the surah moves from Thamud to the people of Lot with the same opening that named the others. “The people of Lot also rejected the warnings,” or “kaththabat qawmu Lootin bin-nuthur.” Ibn Kathir explains that the people of Lot rejected the warnings that their messenger brought, and the believer is taught that the rejection of the warning is the sin that the surah records of every nation, and that the people of Lot are joined to Noah's people and 'Ad and Thamud by the same refusal, so that the recital becomes a single account of the one disease in four peoples. Al-Tabari notes that the surah does not yet describe the sin of Lot's people, for the surah's method is to name the rejection first and to unfold the sin and the punishment after, and the believer is taught to see the rejection as the root and the rest as its fruit.\n\nAl-Razi observes the connection between the nations, for the people of Lot are the fourth of the recital, and the believer is taught that the judgment of God has fallen upon the heedless in every region and every age, and that the people of Lot, whose land was near the land of the Arabs and whose story was known to the Quraysh, are a warning that is close at hand. The verse also teaches the believer the unity of the prophetic warning, for Lot warned his people as Noah and Hud and Salih warned theirs, and the rejection of each warning is the rejection of the one message, and the believer is taught to hear the warnings of the prophets as the one warning, and to respond to it as the one response. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to receive the warning of God through whatever messenger it comes, for the warning is one and the response is one, and the believer is taught to be among those who accept the one warning and are saved from the one punishment.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to see himself in the line of the warned, for the warnings of the prophets reach him through the Quran, and the believer is taught to respond to the warning that has reached him as the ancients were asked to respond to the warning that reached them, and to know that the difference between the saved and the destroyed is the difference of the response.",
  "34": "“We unleashed upon them a storm of stones. As for ˹the believers of˺ Lot’s family, We delivered them before dawn” describes the punishment of Lot's people and the rescue that preceded it, and the verse holds the two together in a single statement. “We unleashed upon them a storm of stones. As for the believers of Lot's family, We delivered them before dawn,” or “inna arsalna 'alayhim hasiban illa ala Lootin najjaynahum bi-sahar.” Ibn Kathir explains that God sent upon the people of Lot a storm of stones — the “hasib” — and the believer is taught that the punishment of Lot's people was the rain of stones that fell upon their cities after they were overturned, and that the same decree which destroyed the wicked spared the household of Lot, who were delivered before dawn, and the believer is taught that the judgment of God distinguishes, and that the rescue of the believers is woven into the destruction of the wicked. Al-Tabari notes the word “illa ala Lootin” — “except the family of Lot” — for the exception is the mercy within the judgment, and the believer is taught that the punishment of a people never sweeps away the faithful who are among them.\n\nAl-Razi observes the detail of the dawn, for the deliverance came before dawn, in the last hours of the night, and the believer is taught that the rescue of God comes at its appointed hour, and that the believer is to trust the timing of the rescue as he trusts the fact of it. The verse also teaches the believer the nature of the punishment of Lot's people, for the stones were sent upon them after their cities were overturned, and the believer is taught that the punishment of the heedless is a punishment that surrounds them, from the earth that overturned beneath them and the sky that rained upon them, and the believer is taught to fear the punishment that leaves no side of escape. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to be among the rescued by being among the faithful, for the rescue of Lot's family was the rescue of the believers, and the believer is taught to cling to the faith that is the condition of the rescue.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to see the dawn of Lot's family as the dawn of every believer, for the rescue of God comes to the faithful in their night, and the believer is taught to wait for his dawn with the trust of one who knows that the Lord of the dawn will not forget him.",
  "35": "“as a blessing from Us. This is how We reward whoever gives thanks.” states the meaning of the rescue, and the verse turns the deliverance of Lot's family into a principle. “As a blessing from Us. This is how We reward whoever gives thanks,” or “ni'matan min 'indina kathalika najzee man shakar.” Ibn Kathir explains that the rescue of Lot's family was a blessing from God, not a wage they had earned, and the believer is taught that the deliverance of the believers is always a grace, for the believer's works do not obligate God, and yet the verse adds that this is the way God rewards those who give thanks, so that the grace is tied to the gratitude, and the believer is taught that the gratitude of the heart is the condition through which the grace is received. Al-Tabari notes the word “shakar” — “he gave thanks” — for the thankfulness that the verse names is the thankfulness of the believer who receives the blessing and returns it in obedience, and the believer is taught that the rescue is given to the thankful and that the thankfulness is the keeping of the rescue.\n\nAl-Razi observes the joining of grace and reward in the verse, for the rescue is called a blessing from God and then called a reward for gratitude, and the believer is taught that the two are not in conflict, for the grace of God precedes and the gratitude of the servant follows, and the reward of gratitude is the increase of the grace, and the believer is taught to receive the blessings of God with gratitude so that the blessings may be made complete. The verse also teaches the believer the measure of thankfulness, for the thankfulness that God rewards is not the thankfulness of the tongue alone but the thankfulness of the heart and the limbs, the thankfulness that turns the blessing into obedience, and the believer is taught to let his gratitude be a life and not a word. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to count the rescues of God in his own life, the dangers averted and the mercies granted, and to answer each with the gratitude that the verse describes, for the grateful are the rewarded.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to make gratitude his habit, for the rescue of Lot's family is the pattern of every rescue, and the believer is taught to know that the one who gives thanks is the one whom God rewards, and that the reward of gratitude is the nearness of God and the increase of His blessing.",
  "36": "“He had already warned them of Our ˹crushing˺ blow but they disputed the warnings.” records the warning that preceded the punishment, and the verse shows that the people of Lot were not destroyed without warning. “He had already warned them of Our crushing blow but they disputed the warnings,” or “wa laqad andharahum batshatana fa-tamaraw bin-nuthur.” Ibn Kathir explains that Lot had warned his people of the “batshah” — the crushing blow, the violent seizure — of God, and the people disputed the warnings, and the believer is taught that the punishment of Lot's people came after a warning that was clear and repeated, and that their ruin was the ruin of the warned who refused. Al-Tabari notes the word “tamaraw” — “they disputed,” “they doubted” — for the people of Lot did not merely ignore the warning but argued against it, and the believer is taught that the disputing of the warning is worse than the ignoring of it, for the disputer has heard and wrestled with the warning and still refused.\n\nAl-Razi observes the lesson of the warning and the dispute, for the people of Lot had been warned of the very punishment that came, and their disputing of the warning did not change the punishment, and the believer is taught that the warning of God is not weakened by the dispute of men, and that the one who disputes the warning disputes a fact that is already fixed. The verse also teaches the believer the mercy of the warning, for the warning was given so that the people might turn, and the believer is taught to receive the warnings of God as the mercies they are, and to turn from the warned-against before the warning is fulfilled. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to warn as Lot warned and to receive as the people of Lot refused, and the believer is taught to know that the dispute of the warned is the last step before the seizure, and that the one who disputes the warning has already half received the punishment.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to heed the warnings that reach him, for the warning is the mercy that stands between him and the blow, and the believer is taught to let the warning work in his heart, and to know that the disputing of the warning is the closing of the door that the warning held open.",
  "37": "“And they even demanded his angel-guests from him, so We blinded their eyes. ˹And they were told,˺ ‘Taste then My punishment and warnings!’” records the final crime of Lot's people, and the verse exposes the depth of their corruption by their own demand. “And they even demanded his angel-guests from him, so We blinded their eyes. And they were told, 'Taste then My punishment and warnings!'” or “wa laqad rawadoohu 'an dayfihi fa-tamasna a'yunahum fa-thooqoo 'athabee wa nuthur.” Ibn Kathir explains that the people of Lot came to Lot's house and demanded that he hand over his guests — the angels who had come in the form of handsome young men — and the believer is taught that the demand was the culmination of their corruption, for they sought to commit their sin upon the guests of the prophet, and the blindness that followed was the punishment that answered the demand. Al-Tabari notes that the angels were the guests whom the people demanded, and the people did not know that their demanded guests were the angels of their destruction, and the believer is taught that the sin which is pursued against the signs of God is the sin that is answered by the sign itself.\n\nAl-Razi observes the word “tamasna” — “We blinded” — for God struck their eyes with blindness, and the commentators relate that the blindness was the punishment that prevented them from carrying out their intent, and the believer is taught that the punishment of God often comes in the form that blocks the sin, for the Lord who sees the sin can blind the sinner to his prey. The verse then records the words spoken to them — “Taste then My punishment and warnings” — for the punishment and the warning are joined in the address, and the believer is taught that the punishment is the taste of what the warning had foretold, and that the one who refused the warning tastes the punishment that the warning described. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to fear the sin that is pursued against the guests and the signs of God, for the demand of the people of Lot was the demand that brought the blindness and the storm, and the believer is taught to honor the signs of God and to know that the violation of the sign is the invocation of the judgment.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to see the blindness of Lot's people as the image of the blindness of every heedless heart, for the sin that is pursued blinds the pursuer to his own ruin, and the believer is taught to seek the sight of faith, which sees the end of the sin before the sin is tasted.",
  "38": "“And indeed, by the early morning they were overwhelmed by an unrelenting torment.” records the completion of the punishment, and the verse places the final blow in the early morning. “And indeed, by the early morning they were overwhelmed by an unrelenting torment,” or “wa laqad sabbahahum bukratan 'athabun mustaqirr.” Ibn Kathir explains that the punishment overtook the people of Lot by the early morning, and the word “mustaqirr” — “unrelenting,” “settled,” “abiding” — describes the torment that did not pass but settled upon them and remained, for the punishment of the people of Lot was not a passing fright but a settled ruin, and the believer is taught that the punishment of God, once it falls, is not lifted, and that the warning which preceded it had offered the only lifting that was possible — the turning before the fall. Al-Tabari notes the word “bukrah” — “early morning” — for the punishment came at the dawn, and the believer is taught that the rescue of Lot's family and the ruin of their people happened in the same hours, the family led out before dawn and the people overtaken at dawn, so that the mercy and the judgment fell side by side.\n\nAl-Razi observes the contrast of the two dawns, for the dawn that delivered the believers was the dawn that destroyed the disbelievers, and the believer is taught that the same hour of God is mercy to one and judgment to another, and that the difference is the difference of the lives that preceded the hour. The word “mustaqirr” teaches the believer the nature of the punishment of the Hereafter as well, for the unrelenting torment of the people of Lot is the foretaste of the unrelenting torment that the Quran warns of, and the believer is taught to flee the settled punishment by the repentance that is accepted while the warning still stands. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to make his morning the morning of the rescued, for the believer who wakes in obedience is the believer whose dawn is mercy, and the believer is taught to begin his days with the remembrance that the dawn of Lot's people was the dawn of their end.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to read the morning as a sign, for the morning that is given to him is a mercy that was withheld from the people of Lot, and the believer is taught to greet each morning with the gratitude of the rescued and the fear of the one who knows that the morning can be the hour of the judgment.",
  "39": "“˹Again they were told,˺ ‘Taste now My punishment and warnings!’” repeats the address that was spoken to the people of Lot, and the verse fixes the word of the punishment in the surah's refrain. “Again they were told, 'Taste now My punishment and warnings!'” or “fa-thooqoo 'athabee wa nuthur.” Ibn Kathir explains that the address is repeated, for the punishment and the warning are joined in the word spoken to the punished, and the believer is taught that the punishment is the taste of what the warning had described, and that the one who refused to heed the warning now tastes the punishment, and the two are one in the word “taste.” Al-Tabari notes the repetition of the address in the surah, for the same word is spoken to the people of Lot that was spoken earlier, and the believer is taught that the address of the punishment is the completion of the address of the warning, and that the two addresses are the two ends of the one divine speech.\n\nAl-Razi observes the word “thooqoo” — “taste” — and explains that the punishment is described as a taste, for the tasting is the direct experience of what the warning had only described, and the believer is taught that there is a distance between hearing the description and tasting the reality, and that the wisdom of the warning is to keep the hearer on the side of the description, for the one who crosses to the side of the taste has crossed the bridge of the warning. The verse also teaches the believer the seriousness of the divine address, for the word “taste” is spoken by God, and the believer is taught to hear the word as the word of the Lord, and to answer it with the turning that makes the taste unnecessary. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to take the warning as the substitute for the punishment, for the warning is the mercy that spares the taste, and the believer is taught to prefer the warning to the taste, and to turn before the address of the warning becomes the address of the punishment.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to hear the word “taste” with the ear of one who is still on the side of the warning, and to know that the word is spoken to the punished so that the warned may never have to hear it spoken to themselves.",
  "40": "“And We have certainly made the Quran easy to remember. So is there anyone who will be mindful?” follows the punishment of Lot's people with the refrain of the Quran's ease, and the verse repeats the remedy for the fourth time. “And We have certainly made the Quran easy to remember. So is there anyone who will be mindful?” or “wa laqad yassarna al-Qur'ana lil-dhikri fa-hal min muddakir.” Ibn Kathir explains that the ease of the Quran is asserted again after the account of Lot's people, and the believer is taught that the Quran is the means by which every fate recited in the surah may be avoided, for the one who takes the Quran to heart remembers and is saved. Al-Tabari notes that the refrain has now been repeated after each of the four nations, and the believer is taught that the repetition is the mercy of God, who does not weary of reminding, and that the believer is taught to not weary of being reminded.\n\nAl-Razi observes the design of the surah, for the four accounts and the four refrains are the structure of the chapter, and the believer is taught to read the surah as a single call — the call of the punishment and the call of the ease — and to hear in the fourfold refrain the fourfold mercy that follows the fourfold warning. The word “lil-dhikri” — “for remembrance” — is the purpose of the ease, and the believer is taught that the Quran is made easy so that the remembrance may be possible, and that the remembrance is the answer to the question “is there anyone who will be mindful,” and the believer is taught to be the one who answers. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to make the Quran his refuge, for the surah has shown him the fates of the heedless, and the Quran is the refuge that the heedless refused, and the believer is taught to enter the refuge and to remain within it.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to answer the refrain with the act of remembrance, for the ease of the Quran is the gift that the surah offers after each warning, and the believer is taught to take the gift, and to know that the taking of the gift is the difference between the one who is warned and saved and the one who is warned and destroyed.",
  "41": "“And indeed, the warnings ˹also˺ came to the people of Pharaoh.” turns to the fifth and final nation of the recital, and the surah moves from Lot's people to the people of Pharaoh. “And indeed, the warnings also came to the people of Pharaoh,” or “wa laqad ja'a ala Fir'awna an-nuthur.” Ibn Kathir explains that the warnings came to the people of Pharaoh through Moses and Aaron, and the believer is taught that the people of Pharaoh are the last of the recital, and that the warnings that came to them were the warnings of Moses, supported by the signs and the plagues, so that no people in the recital received a fuller warning than they. Al-Tabari notes that the verse says “the warnings came to the people of Pharaoh” — the warning is said to have come, without yet naming their response — and the believer is taught that the coming of the warning is the mercy, and that the response to the warning is the judgment, and that the next verse records the response and its consequence.\n\nAl-Razi observes the place of Pharaoh's people in the recital, for they are the mightiest of the nations named, the people of a kingdom and an army and a river, and their might did not avail them, and the believer is taught that the power of the heedless is not a protection, and that the warnings of God reach the palaces as they reach the tents, and that the palace is no refuge from the decree. The verse also teaches the believer the completeness of the divine warning, for the people of Pharaoh were warned by Moses with the staff and the hand and the plagues, and the believer is taught that the warning of God is given with every proof, so that the one who refuses is left without excuse, and the believer is taught to receive the warnings that come to him with the proofs that accompany them. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to see the palace of Pharaoh as the image of every worldly stronghold, and to know that the stronghold which the warnings reach is the stronghold that the judgment can enter.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to hear the warnings that have come to him, for the warnings that came to Pharaoh's people have come to every people through the Quran, and the believer is taught to respond to the warning that has reached his own ear, and to know that the warning which is received is the mercy, and the warning which is refused is the proof.",
  "42": "“˹But˺ they rejected all of Our signs, so We seized them with the ˹crushing˺ grip of the Almighty, Most Powerful.” closes the recital of the five nations with the fate of Pharaoh's people, and the verse ends the surah's history with the grip that gathered them all. “But they rejected all of Our signs, so We seized them with the crushing grip of the Almighty, Most Powerful,” or “kaththaboo bi-ayatina kulliha fa-akhadhnahum akhtha 'azeezin muqtadir.” Ibn Kathir explains that the people of Pharaoh rejected all the signs of God — the staff, the hand, the plagues, the nine signs — and the word “kulliha” — “all of them” — is emphatic, for their rejection was total, and the believer is taught that the rejection of all the signs is the rejection that calls down the complete seizure, and the seizure is described as the grip of the “'Azeez,” the Almighty, and the “Muqtadir,” the Most Powerful, so that the punishment of Pharaoh's people is the punishment of the power of God meeting the power of man. Al-Tabari notes the word “akhth” — “seizure,” “grip” — for the punishment is described as a grip, the seizing of the mighty by the Mightier, and the believer is taught that the power of the tyrant is nothing in the grip of the Lord of power.\n\nAl-Razi observes the fittingness of the closing, for the surah began with the sign of the moon and has recited the nations in order, and it ends with the mightiest of them all, seized by the grip of the Almighty, and the believer is taught that the whole recital is the proof of the one lesson — that the signs of God are true and the rejection of them is ruin — and that the grip which seized Pharaoh's people is the grip that awaits every people who repeat their rejection. The two names — “'Azeez” and “Muqtadir” — are the answer to every boast of power, for the power of God is the power that no power can meet, and the believer is taught to fear the Almighty and the Most Powerful, and to take refuge in His mercy from His grip. Al-Qurtubi draws out the lesson that the believer is taught to close his reading of the recital with the fear that the surah intends, and to know that the grip that seized the five nations is the grip that the Quran warns of, and that the refuge from the grip is the response to the warning.\n\nAl-Sa'di observes that the believer is taught to let the recital of the surah stand before him as a single warning, for the five nations and the five grips are one account of the one law, and the believer is taught to place himself on the side of the warned who respond, and to know that the Almighty and Most Powerful is the one who seizes the rejecters and saves the responders, and the believer is taught to be among the saved.",
  "43": "“Are you Meccan disbelievers superior to those destroyed peoples?” asks whether Quraysh possess greater power, morality, or divine favor than Noah’s people, 'Ad, Thamud, Lot’s people, and Pharaoh’s regime. Those nations often had greater material strength, so Meccan confidence had no historical basis.\n\n“Or have you been granted immunity from punishment in divine Books?” No revealed scripture gave them an exemption. A true immunity would require authorization from Allah, not tribal pride or residence near the Sacred Mosque. Sacred location increases responsibility; it does not protect defiance automatically.\n\nThe questions remove two false shelters: superior ability and promised exemption. They had neither. History showed power fails, and revelation supplied no guarantee.\n\nBelievers should hear this personally. Membership in a Muslim community, family, or sacred institution is not immunity while truth is knowingly violated. Faith and repentance matter. At the same time, humans should not predict a specific nation’s destruction without revelation. The verse addresses Quraysh through a confirmed prophetic warning. Its general principle is humility: no group should assume moral laws apply only to predecessors. Ask what evidence supports confidence before Allah. If the answer is identity or worldly strength, the earlier histories have already removed it.\n\nThe phrase “divine Books” also establishes scripture as the only possible source for a true exemption in the unseen. Political treaties, priestly assurances, and cultural myths cannot issue immunity from Allah. None of the earlier books promised that Quraysh could reject the final Messenger safely. This principle protects people from anyone selling guaranteed salvation without revealed evidence. Hope rests on Allah’s promises—faith, repentance, mercy—not a document or affiliation invented by people. A believer may hold certificates and honors, but none accompanies the soul as immunity. The relevant book at judgment is the record formed through belief and deeds. The question therefore dismantles inherited entitlement and directs every generation back to proof. Revelation, not ancestry, defines the safe path, and sincere submission remains open to anyone who turns to Allah.",
  "44": "“We are all a united front” records Quraysh’s confidence in collective power. Tribal alliances, fighters, wealth, and shared interest made them think Muhammad’s followers could be overwhelmed. Unity appeared to guarantee correctness and success.\n\n“Bound to prevail” turns confidence into certainty. They treated human organization as though it controlled outcome. Yet unity around falsehood does not make falsehood true, and numbers do not overrule Allah.\n\nThe verse does not condemn unity itself. Believers are commanded to cooperate in righteousness and avoid division. The question is what unites people and which methods they use. A united front around injustice can multiply harm and accountability.\n\nModern communities may repeat the boast through military coalitions, political majorities, corporate scale, or online crowds. These are real means and should be assessed, but none is “bound” to prevail. Believers also avoid the opposite boast about their own groups; victory has moral and divine conditions, not merely a religious label. Build unity through truth, consultation, justice, and humility. Collective strength is safest when it knows it remains created. Quraysh’s boast will be answered in the next verse by a defeat their calculations did not foresee.\n\nA united front can also suppress internal conscience. Individuals may doubt the group’s wrongdoing yet remain silent because collective certainty feels safer. Quraysh’s boast made defection appear foolish. The Quran gives moral room to step away before the front flees. Believers should create communities where sincere correction is welcomed and unity is never purchased through concealment. Consultation tests assumptions; loyalty to Allah outranks loyalty to faction. The strongest unity is capable of repentance. A group unable to admit wrong may be tightly bound but spiritually brittle, because its cohesion depends on resisting the truth that could heal it. Their number did not prevent panic once Allah’s decree arrived. Moral courage begins before crisis, when a person is willing to examine the shared claim instead of borrowing confidence from the crowd.",
  "45": "“Their united front will be defeated” foretells the collapse of Quraysh’s collective confidence. The promise was fulfilled notably at the Battle of Badr, where a larger, better-equipped Meccan force was defeated. Revelation announced the reversal before worldly evidence made it obvious.\n\n“And forced to flee” makes defeat visible. The front claiming inevitable victory would turn its backs and leave the battlefield. Numbers and organization remained real, but Allah’s decree exposed their limits.\n\nThis prediction strengthened believers without granting permission for arrogance. Badr involved lawful conflict under the Prophet’s leadership and specific divine support. Later groups cannot assume every campaign is guaranteed the same result merely by quoting the verse.\n\nThe moral pattern still warns unjust coalitions. Unity cannot secure a cause against truth forever. Believers use strategy and preparation but refuse to worship them. They also distinguish confidence from presumption: hope in Allah accompanies self-review, obedience, and ethical limits. If success comes, gratitude belongs to Him; if a setback comes, identity alone does not cancel the need to learn. Quraysh’s fleeing showed that the loudest forecast may be the least stable when it ignores the Lord of outcomes.\n\nBadr’s fulfillment also shows that prophecy can name a worldly event without making Islam dependent solely upon military outcome. The Quran’s message was true before battle and remained true through later trials. Victory served as one sign, not the definition of every faithful day. Communities should not turn history into triumphalism or ignore the lives lost on all sides. The Prophet’s conduct after victories remained governed by mercy and law. When Allah reverses power, believers face a new test: will they remain humble and just, or copy the arrogance whose prediction failed? Badr did not authorize aggression; it occurred after persecution and expulsion, under prophetic command and revealed limits. Its lesson is confidence in Allah, not a license to attach sacred certainty to every political conflict.",
  "46": "“The Hour is their appointed time” moves beyond Badr and every worldly defeat. Even if some opponents escape temporal consequence, none escapes resurrection. The final appointment is personal, comprehensive, and set by Allah.\n\n“Better yet” means the Hour is greater, more decisive, and more fitting as the full answer to their rejection. Worldly battle cannot recompense every hidden motive or harm. Final judgment can.\n\n“The Hour will be most catastrophic and most bitter.” Its upheaval exceeds battlefield fear, and its consequence tastes bitter to those who denied. The repeated “most” removes any idea that temporary loss is the whole warning.\n\nBelievers should not delight in another’s catastrophe. They take warning, seek refuge, and invite people while repentance remains possible. The appointed time is hidden, preventing both scheduled delay and manipulation by date-setters. Personal death may arrive before the cosmic Hour and closes the individual field of work. Preparation therefore belongs now: establish faith, restore rights, and refuse the confidence that one worldly escape settles the account. Allah’s final appointment cannot be postponed by a united front or avoided by fleeing.\n\nBitterness arises because the deniers meet both deserved consequence and irreversible regret. Worldly bitterness can pass or be treated; this one belongs to an appointment after action. The Quran mentions it so regret can occur early enough to be useful. A person who feels remorse today should not sink into despair. Remorse can become repentance, restitution, and sweetness of return. The final Hour is catastrophic, but present warning is compassionate. Believers use the contrast to choose difficult truth now over bitter certainty later. Every accepted correction reduces the reasons for fear. Delay should never be confused with cancellation. The appointed Hour gives every ordinary hour urgency, while its hidden date prevents calculations from replacing obedience. Preparing means faithful daily action rather than sensational prediction.",
  "47": "“The wicked are entrenched in misguidance” describes people whose crime and rejection have become settled. They are not merely confused for a moment. Persistent desire, denial, and wrongdoing place them deep within a path away from truth.\n\n“And are bound for blazes.” Misguidance has a destination. The plural or intensive image of fire conveys severe punishment after the appointed Hour. Their path and end correspond; what burns conscience and society through wickedness leads toward actual burning.\n\nThe verse does not allow people to call every sinner permanently wicked. A believer may commit sin, repent, and return. Allah knows who becomes entrenched and who is struggling toward Him. Human advice should keep the door open while naming wrong accurately.\n\nPersonally, the warning is to interrupt sin before it becomes identity. Repetition changes what feels normal, companions reinforce direction, and excuses harden. Prayer, repentance, changed environment, and support can loosen that entrenchment by Allah’s mercy. Fear of blazes should move the heart, not produce hopelessness. The Quran reveals the endpoint precisely so living people can leave the road. No one is “bound” there against sincere repentance while its time remains.\n\nMisguidance and blazes are joined, but Allah never wrongs a sincere seeker. He guides those who turn and provides signs, conscience, revelation, and teachers. Entrenchment develops through repeated refusal, while even deep habits can be addressed through repentance and support. Communities should make return practical: offer confidential counsel, treatment for addiction, and companionship that does not celebrate sin. Boundaries still matter, especially where others are harmed. Mercy is not denial of wickedness; it is an open route out. The verse names the final path clearly so current helpers know the stakes without pretending to own the verdict. Calling conduct wicked does not require delight in anyone’s punishment. The Prophet warned with compassion and wanted people guided. Believers likewise oppose evil firmly while hoping that living wrongdoers repent before meeting the blaze.",
  "48": "“The Day they will be dragged into the Fire on their faces” depicts extreme humiliation and helplessness. Faces once held high in denial meet the ground while angels carry out Allah’s sentence. No status or physical resistance alters the direction.\n\n“Taste the touch of Hell!” makes punishment immediate and personally experienced. “Taste” can describe even a beginning, while Hell’s touch is already overwhelming. The people called warning old magic; direct experience ends that label.\n\nThis scene belongs to final judgment after perfect proof. It never authorizes humans to drag, burn, or humiliate people they classify as wicked. Human law protects dignity and follows evidence even when punishing crime.\n\nThe proper response is inward. Lower the face willingly in prostration before it is lowered in punishment. Use the face for honest speech, compassionate attention, and modesty rather than arrogance. Believers ask Allah to protect them and avoid speaking of Hell with entertainment or superiority. Every living wrongdoer can repent; the dragged people are those whose record has closed in persistence. Severe imagery is merciful when it prevents that end. It becomes distorted when used to justify cruelty now.\n\nBeing dragged “on their faces” also reverses the ordinary use of the face to orient movement. Judgment removes self-direction from people who used choice against guidance. This makes current agency precious. Turn the face toward the Sacred Mosque, lower it in prayer, and use eyes and speech under Allah’s limits. Bodily worship trains orientation before the Day. The scene also reminds believers never to degrade prisoners or enemies by imitating final punishment. Allah’s angels act after perfect judgment; humans remain tested by how they treat even those accused. Moral restraint is part of preparing one’s own face for honor. The direct command “Taste the touch of Hell!” answers the earlier taste for ridicule with terrible reality. The warning encourages control of speech now, when mockery can still be replaced by truthful acknowledgment and prayer.",
  "49": "“We have created everything” affirms that no object, event, ability, or law exists independently of Allah’s creative will. He alone is uncreated. Human actions occur through capacities and circumstances He creates, while humans genuinely choose and are accountable as revelation repeatedly teaches.\n\n“Perfectly preordained” means everything has measure, proportion, timing, and decree. Creation is not an uncontrolled accident. Bodies, stars, lifespans, provision, and events unfold within knowledge written before their occurrence.\n\nDivine preordination does not excuse sin. The same Quran that affirms decree condemns wicked choices and records deeds. People do not know the hidden decree before acting; they know commands, abilities, and consequences. After an event, decree gives patience without rewriting wrong as right.\n\nBelievers combine action and reliance. Plan, choose lawful means, pray, and accept outcomes beyond control. Avoid fatalism that abandons effort and pride that imagines effort creates independently. “Perfectly” also comforts people facing chaos: human sight may not understand the measure, but nothing escaped knowledge. Trust can coexist with grief and attempts to change what remains changeable. The Creator’s decree is wise, while the servant’s duty is obedience within the moment given.\n\nPreordination also applies to the measured creation in the next chapter: sun, moon, sky, balance, and human form. Qadar is not only about unexpected tragedy; it includes regular beauty and proportion. Remembering it during ease creates gratitude before hardship demands patience. The doctrine should never become fuel for anxious speculation about hidden fate. Muhammad taught action, prayer, and trust. People do not read the record in advance; they respond to commands in the present. What has passed is met with faith and learning, while what remains is approached through responsible choice. This is the balanced fruit of belief in decree. Human planning belongs inside this measured order. Make plans, take causes, and seek excellence, but do not worship control. Results may differ from intention without proving that wise effort was pointless or that Allah abandoned the servant.",
  "50": "“Our command is but a single word” communicates the ease and unity of Allah’s execution. He does not need tools, assistants, repeated attempts, or physical exertion. When He wills a created event, His command brings it according to decree.\n\n“Done in the blink of an eye” uses one of the fastest familiar human moments to describe immediate fulfillment. The comparison conveys speed without limiting Allah to biological blinking. His command can be faster than human perception.\n\nThis power explains resurrection, destroyed nations, and every sign in the chapter. Gathering all creation is no harder for Him than one act. Delay therefore reflects wisdom and appointed measure, never inability.\n\nBelievers should not use the verse to demand instant answers to every prayer. Allah can act immediately, but chooses timing through knowledge. His ability gives hope; His wisdom gives patience. Human tasks still require planning and steps because servants operate within created processes. They should not imitate divine speech by making careless snap decisions. The lesson is dependence: after using every lawful means, one command can open or close what no human could control. Nothing is too complex for the Lord’s single word.\n\nThe single command also prevents imagining conflict within divine will. Created agents may resist His moral command and earn sin, but they never escape His sovereign ability. Their resistance itself exists only for the test and ends when He commands final judgment. This should humble tyrants and reassure victims. Yet believers should not demand that Allah exercise immediate power according to their timetable. The One capable of instant action may delay for repentance, proof, or wisdom. Patience trusts both speed and delay because each belongs to the same Lord. His command is effortless; His timing is never careless. Its likeness to a blink communicates speed in language people understand, not physical limitation. Allah does not require eyes, distance, machinery, or elapsed effort. Creation’s fastest familiar motion only points toward His incomparable ease.",
  "51": "“We have already destroyed the likes of you” addresses Quraysh through earlier peoples similar in denial, power, and mockery. The precise cultures differed, but the moral type was familiar. History had already demonstrated that no opposing society is unprecedented to Allah.\n\n“Likes of you” removes the comfort of imagined uniqueness. People often think earlier warnings applied to primitive strangers while their own sophistication creates exemption. Revelation identifies repeated human motives beneath changing technology.\n\n“Will any of you be mindful?” asks for one different response. The past cannot repent, but present listeners can learn. Mindfulness sees similarity before consequence and chooses another path.\n\nThe verse does not let believers claim every modern opponent is exactly one destroyed people. Similarity must be established carefully, and specific punishment cannot be predicted without revelation. The personal warning is safer and immediate: where do I resemble pride, desire, or resistance described? Historical comparison should humble, not arm arrogance. Allah preserved the likeness so it would not have to become destiny. One mindful person can break a pattern generations repeated.\n\n“Already” makes history available evidence rather than a threat with no precedent. Allah does not ask them to imagine whether powerful deniers can fall; examples have occurred and been narrated. Present people possess even more accumulated history. This increases opportunity and responsibility. Yet awareness should not produce superiority over the dead. The same traits can live in the reader. Mindfulness asks how to become unlike them through humility, prayer, fair dealings, and response to warning. The verse’s question is not answered by saying “yes” but by a life that demonstrates remembered difference. The words “the likes of you” make resemblance moral, not necessarily ethnic or technological. Whenever arrogance and denial return in new clothing, the old warning remains relevant. Surviving today is not proof of permanent safety.",
  "52": "“Everything they have done is listed in their records” affirms comprehensive documentation of human action. The pronoun first refers to deniers, but the principle of recorded deeds applies to every accountable person. Nothing disappears because people forget or institutions erase it.\n\n“Records” are maintained by angels under Allah’s command. He does not need them to learn; they manifest order, evidence, and justice. Their unseen format should not be imagined through modern files, though the concept of reliable documentation is clear.\n\nA record includes action and the context Allah commands to be written. Intention is fully known by Him. Public appearance cannot edit the final list.\n\nBelievers can respond through repentance, which Allah may use to erase or transform sin according to revelation, and through adding good. They should also maintain honest earthly records where rights depend on contracts, debts, or testimony. Human documentation remains fallible; divine record does not. This comforts unseen service and warns hidden abuse. A deed’s privacy changes its audience, not its existence before Allah. The record is being formed now, when correction remains possible.\n\nListing deeds also means no person can rewrite a lifetime through one final public narrative. Histories written by victors, families, or critics may distort, but the angelic record remains. This brings relief to those whose names were slandered and warning to those whose image was carefully managed. Believers should still pursue accurate earthly history and testimony because people’s rights matter now. They simply do not mistake human archives for the final truth. Repentance is the one divinely authorized way the moral record can change, and it requires honesty rather than reputation management. The plural “records” points to comprehensive preservation. Whatever details people forget are not lost to Allah. This frees conscience from pretending innocence through failed memory and encourages prompt written accountability in serious human trusts.",
  "53": "“Every matter, small and large, is written precisely” removes the assumption that minor acts escape or major acts overwhelm the record. Scale does not create a blind spot. A word, glance, payment, public policy, and act of violence each receive their proper place.\n\n“Precisely” means no exaggeration, omission, or confusion. Human records may lose nuance; Allah knows intention, effect, ability, and context. Writing establishes truth under His perfect judgment.\n\nSmall acts should neither be despised nor obsessively feared without mercy. A smile, removed harm, or quiet remembrance can carry reward. A minor sin calls for repentance, while repeated carelessness may form character. Major sins require urgent sincere return and repair.\n\nThe verse supports balanced accountability. Do not assume a large charitable act cancels every private injustice or that one forgiven small lapse makes hope impossible. Review the full direction of life and rely on Allah’s forgiveness while acting responsibly. Precise writing means every improvement matters. The smallest turn back is seen, and the largest public image cannot hide what lies beneath. Human beings should be similarly careful with people’s rights without pretending their judgment equals the final record.\n\nSmall and large can also refer to matters people classify differently from Allah’s scale. A quiet word may save a life; a celebrated project may carry hidden harm. Precision includes consequence as well as appearance. This should make believers thoughtful but not paralyzed. Learn clear obligations, intend good, consult where stakes are high, and ask forgiveness for errors. Scrupulous anxiety that treats every neutral act as catastrophic is not the verse’s purpose. Perfect writing belongs to a merciful, just Lord. Responsibility can therefore be serious without becoming hopeless, because repentance and accepted good are written precisely too. The book’s completeness includes intentions insofar as Allah has taught the angels to record and judge them. Hidden sincerity can magnify a modest deed, while showing off can hollow out a grand display. Allah alone knows hearts perfectly.",
  "54": "“The righteous will be amid Gardens and rivers” shifts from records and Hell to the reward of taqwa. Righteous people feared Allah, guarded His limits, and returned after mistakes. Their final surroundings display life, beauty, abundance, and peace.\n\n“Gardens” offer permanent dwelling rather than temporary scenery. “Rivers” flow without drought, pollution, or competition. What required labor and scarcity in worldly life arrives as uninterrupted gift.\n\nThe contrast with wicked people is moral, not ethnic or social. Anyone can seek righteousness through faith and good action, and entry remains through Allah’s mercy. Wealth cannot buy these Gardens; poverty cannot block them.\n\nBelievers should let hope energize restraint. Every forbidden appetite left for Allah protects access to purer enjoyment. Every garden and river now can inspire gratitude while reminding people to care for earthly water and land. Stewardship does not earn Paradise independently, but reflects awareness of the giver. The righteous are not merely kept from Fire; they are placed amid positive and beautiful abundance. Divine religion calls toward flourishing, not deprivation for its own sake.\n\nRivers also symbolize movement without danger. The flood’s water destroyed deniers; Paradise water serves life eternally. Creation’s moral role changes by Allah’s decree. The righteous do not fear a river becoming punishment or drying into loss. This complete security is part of reward. Current water should remind believers of both dependence and hope. Conserve it, make it accessible, and thank Allah. Earthly stewardship cannot create the heavenly river, but it can express the taqwa that seeks it. The same Lord who measured worldly water promises abundance without anxiety for His righteous servants. Gardens add shelter, beauty, food, fragrance, and peaceful company to the rivers. Reward addresses the whole person rather than offering a bare escape from pain. Taqwa’s restraints lead to a life richer than forbidden appetite promised.",
  "55": "“At the Seat of Honour” describes a position of truth, dignity, security, and accepted standing. No lie, humiliation, or unstable claim enters this seat. Those whose records were faithful receive an honor worldly status could not guarantee.\n\n“In the presence of the Most Powerful Sovereign” expresses nearness, favor, and honored relation to Allah. It does not mean He is contained like a human king within a room. His presence and nearness are understood in a manner fitting His majesty.\n\n“Most Powerful” assures no one can remove the inhabitants, while “Sovereign” means every kingdom belongs to Him. Their security is permanent because its guarantor cannot be overthrown.\n\nThe verse combines truth and power. Human seats may possess power without honesty or honorable claims without ability to protect. Allah’s kingdom unites perfect justice with unstoppable authority. Believers seek this seat through truthful speech, covenant, and worship, not through flattering earthly rulers. Worldly office becomes a test; the eternal seat is a reward. The chapter closes with the deniers’ united front gone and the righteous near the real Sovereign. That is the final reversal by which every temporary claim is measured.\n\nThe Seat of Honour is also a “seat of truth” in the underlying expression. Nothing there is promised falsely or maintained by propaganda. In this world, closeness to power can require compromise; in the Hereafter, closeness to the Sovereign confirms righteousness rather than corrupting it. This gives believers a standard for ambition. Seek positions where truth can be served, not where honor depends on hiding it. If office threatens faith, eternal honor outweighs temporary seating. The most secure audience is with the King who already knows every private intention and needs no flattery. “Most Powerful” guarantees that no rival can remove the honored guests or overturn the verdict. “Sovereign” means His authority is complete and rightful. Nearness here is safety granted by the One whose power needs no oppression."
};
